sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To cut a given straight line so that the rectangle contained by the whole and one of the segments equals the square on the remaining segment.
----
proof:

Let {AB} be the given straight line.

It is required to cut {AB} so that the rectangle contained by the whole and one of the segments equals the square on the remaining segment.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="280" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img src="propII11.gif" alt="II.11"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The first construction in Book II, and the book's most cited
    // proposition — the golden section, stated before ratios exist.
    //
    // Joyce's figure draws the two squares but none of the three regions
    // the proof names by their diagonal corners: FK (the rectangle CF by
    // FA), AK (the piece subtracted from both wholes), and HD (the
    // rectangle AB by BH). It also draws several named segments only as
    // polygon edges. Everything added here is fully transparent
    // (;0;0;0 lines, ;0;0;0;0 polygons), so the static figure is
    // unchanged; the names exist only so a slide can light them.
    //
    // Colour: Joyce's two random fills stay. The containing figures are
    // the unfilled ones — FK is FH + AK, and AD is AK + HD — so each
    // whole is left unfilled and its own parts fill it. The equalities
    // are carried by the gold highlight and the outline animations.
    //
    // Every lettered point the proof introduces mid-walk (D, C, E, F, H,
    // G, K) is a DERIVED construction — vertex / last / midpoint /
    // extend — so none of them is auto-unioned into every slide and
    // deferDraggables has nothing to defer. The opposite rule bites
    // instead: derived points follow `visible`, so each is listed
    // explicitly in every slide it belongs to. The only draggables are
    // A and B (the given line, introduced on slide 1) and the colourless
    // carriers A0/A3.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","AB"];
    var SQ    = GIVEN.concat(["C","D","BD","AC","ABDC","E","AE","BE"]);
    var BUILT = SQ.concat(["F","CF","AF","EF","FAHG","G","H","FG","GH",
                           "HK","K","AH","BH"]);
    var CUT   = BUILT.concat(["FGKC","AHKC","HBDK"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "II.11",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,120;0,0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,120;0,0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,50,100",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,190,100",
            "BD;line;perpendicular;B,A;0;0;0",
            "D;point;last;BD",
            "ABDC;polygon;parallelogram;A,B,D;0;0;0,0,0;random",
            "C;point;vertex;ABDC,4",
            "E;point;midpoint;A,C",
            "BE;line;connect;B,E",
            "F;point;extend;C,E,E,B",
            "FAHG;polygon;square;F,A;0;0;0,0,0;random",
            "H;point;vertex;FAHG,3",
            "G;point;vertex;FAHG,4",
            "HK;line;parallel;H,A,C",
            "K;point;last;HK",
            // Hover / highlight targets only — the squares and the lines
            // above already draw every one of these segments.
            "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;0",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E;0;0;0",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F;0;0;0",
            "CF;line;connect;C,F;0;0;0",
            "AF;line;connect;A,F;0;0;0",
            "AH;line;connect;A,H;0;0;0",
            "BH;line;connect;B,H;0;0;0",
            "FG;line;connect;F,G;0;0;0",
            "GH;line;connect;G,H;0;0;0",
            // The three regions the proof names by diagonal corners: FK,
            // AK, HD. Explicit ;0 faces — an omitted faceColor is an
            // OPAQUE near-white face, and a highlighted polygon is
            // promoted within its draw pass (#140), so an implicit face
            // would white out the squares underneath at exactly the
            // payoff slide.
            "FGKC;polygon;quadrilateral;F,G,K,C;0;0;0;0",
            "AHKC;polygon;quadrilateral;A,H,K,C;0;0;0;0",
            "HBDK;polygon;quadrilateral;H,B,D,K;0;0;0;0",
            // The one angle this proof needs: the right angle at A that
            // lets I.47 turn triangle BAE into squares. Markers are
            // hidden in the static figure (showAngles defaults false),
            // so Euclid's arc-free diagram is untouched.
            "angBAE;sector;angleMarker;A,B,E;0;0;red;red"
        ],
        aliases: { "BA": "AB", "CA": "AC", "FA": "AF", "EA": "AE",
                   "FE": "EF", "EB": "BE", "HA": "AH", "HB": "BH",
                   "GF": "FG", "HG": "GH", "DB": "BD",
                   // Joyce names each region by its diagonal corners.
                   "AD": "ABDC", "FH": "FAHG", "FK": "FGKC",
                   "AK": "AHKC", "HD": "HBDK",
                   "BAE": "angBAE", "EAB": "angBAE" },
        slides: [
            { text: "Let {AB} be the given straight line.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB"] },
            { text: "It is required to cut {AB} so that the rectangle contained by the whole and one of the segments equals the square on the remaining segment.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB"] },
            { text: "Describe the square {ABDC} on {AB}. Bisect {AC} at the point {E}, and join {BE}.",
              visible: SQ,
              // Deliberately NOT highlighted: these are the animation's
              // own targets. SlateAnimator hides an animated target until
              // its own cascade step starts, but sets shouldHighlight at
              // run start, and drawFace renders a hidden element that is
              // highlighted — so listing them here paints the square
              // filled from t=0. The animation is the emphasis.
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "ABDC", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "E",    name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "BE",   name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "the square ABDC on AB", ref: "I.46" },
                  { claim: "AC bisected at E",      ref: "I.10" },
                  { ref: "I.Post.1" } ] },
            { text: "Draw {CA} through to {F}, and make {EF} equal to {BE}. Describe the square {FH} on {AF}, and draw {GH} through to {K}.",
              visible: BUILT,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "CF",   name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend },
                  { elem: "F",    name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "FAHG", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "HK",   name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { ref: "I.Post.2" },
                  { claim: "EF cut off equal to BE", ref: "I.3" },
                  { claim: "the square FH on AF",    ref: "I.46" } ] },
            { text: "I say that {AB} has been cut at {H} so that the rectangle {AB} by {BH} equals the square on {AH}.",
              visible: BUILT,
              highlighted: ["AB","H","BH","AH"] },
            { text: "Since the straight line {AC} has been bisected at {E}, and {FA} is added to it, the rectangle {CF} by {FA} together with the square on {AE} equals the square on {EF}.",
              visible: BUILT,
              highlighted: ["AC","E","CF","AF","AE","EF"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "rectangle CF by FA plus square on AE equals square on EF", ref: "II.6" } ] },
            { text: "But {EF} equals {EB}, therefore the rectangle {CF} by {FA} together with the square on {AE} equals the square on {EB}.",
              visible: BUILT,
              highlighted: ["EF","BE","CF","AF","AE"] },
            { text: "But the sum of the squares on {BA} and {AE} equals the square on {EB}, for the angle at {A} is right, therefore the rectangle {CF} by {FA} together with the square on {AE} equals the sum of the squares on {BA} and {AE}.",
              visible: BUILT.concat(["angBAE"]),
              highlighted: ["AB","AE","BE"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angBAE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "square on EB equals squares on BA and AE", ref: "I.47" } ] },
            { text: "Subtract the square on {AE} from each. Therefore the remaining rectangle {CF} by {FA} equals the square on {AB}.",
              visible: BUILT.concat(["angBAE"]),
              highlighted: ["CF","AF","AB","AE"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "subtract the square on AE", ref: "C.N.3" } ] },
            { text: "Now the rectangle {CF} by {FA} is {FK}, for {AF} equals {FG}, and the square on {AB} is {AD}, therefore {FK} equals {AD}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["ABDC","AF","FG"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "FGKC", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "AF equals FG, sides of the square FH", ref: "I.Def.22" } ] },
            { text: "Subtract {AK} from each. Therefore {FH}, which remains, equals {HD}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["FAHG","HBDK"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "AHKC", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "subtract the common part AK", ref: "C.N.3" } ] },
            { text: "And {HD} is the rectangle {AB} by {BH}, for {AB} equals {BD}, and {FH} is the square on {AH}, therefore the rectangle {AB} by {BH} equals the square on {HA}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["HBDK","AB","BD","BH","FAHG","AH"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "AB equals BD, sides of the square AD", ref: "I.Def.22" },
                  { claim: "HD is the rectangle contained by AB and BH", ref: "II.Def.1" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore the given straight line {AB} has been cut at {H} so that the rectangle {AB} by {BH} equals the square on {HA}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["AB","H","HBDK","FAHG"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.F." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.3":       "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI3/",
        "I.10":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI10/",
        "I.46":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI46/",
        "I.47":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI47/",
        "I.Def.22":  "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI22/",
        "I.Post.1":  "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.Post.2":  "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post2/",
        "C.N.3":     "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn3/",
        "II.6":      "/elements/books/bookII/propositions/propII6/",
        "II.Def.1":  "/elements/books/bookII/definitions/defII1/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Describe the square {ABDC} on {AB}.  Bisect {AC} at the point {E}, and join {BE}. [!just I.46, I.10]

Draw {CA} through to {F}, and make {EF} equal to {BE}.  Describe the square {FH} on {AF}, and draw {GH} through to {K}. [!just I.3; I.46]

I say that {AB} has been cut at {H} so that the rectangle {AB} by {BH} equals the square on {AH}.

Since the straight line {AC} has been bisected at {E}, and {FA} is added to it, the rectangle {CF} by {FA} together with the square on {AE} equals the square on {EF}. [!just II.6]

But {EF} equals {EB}, therefore the rectangle {CF} by {FA} together with the square on {AE} equals the square on {EB}.

But the sum of the squares on {BA} and {AE} equals the square on {EB}, for the angle at {A} is right,  therefore the rectangle {CF} by {FA} together with the square on {AE} equals the sum of the squares on {BA} and {AE}. [!just I.47]

Subtract the square on {AE}  from each. Therefore the remaining rectangle {CF} by {FA} equals the square on {AB}.

Now the rectangle {CF} by {FA} is {FK}, for {AF} equals {FG}, and the square on {AB} is {AD}, therefore {FK} equals {AD}.

Subtract {AK} from each. Therefore {FH}, which remains, equals {HD}.

And {HD} is the rectangle {AB} by {BH}, for {AB} equals {BD}, and {FH} is the square on {AH}, therefore the rectangle  {AB} by {BH} equals the square on {HA}.

Therefore the given straight line {AB} has been cut at {H} so that the rectangle  {AB} by {BH}  equals the square on {HA}.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
----
guide:

This construction cuts a line into two parts to solve the equation <span class="math">a\,(a - x) = x^2</span> geometrically.

This construction is used in the proof of @IV.10, which is later used to construct a regular pentagon.  It accomplishes the same thing as the construction of proposition @VI.30, which cuts a line into extreme and mean ratio, defined in @VI.Def.3, and that construction is used
later in @XIII.17.

The difference between this proposition and VI.30 is a matter of terminology.  Propositions dealing with ratios of lines are postponed until Book VI, but any ratio concerning lines can be converted into a statement about areas of rectangles.  Proposition @VI.16 states that the line *A* is to the line *B* as the line *C* is to the line *D* is equivalent to the statement that the rectangle *A* by *D* equals the rectangle *B* by *C.*

The construction of this proposition cuts a line into two parts *A* and *B* so that the rectangle *A* + *B* by *A* equals the square *B* by *B.*  The construction in VI.30 cuts a line so that <span class="math">A + B : B = B : A</span>, which by VI.16, or by its special case @VI.17, is the same thing.

#### Construction steps

<figure class="diagram rdiagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="280" height="360" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img src="propII11a.gif" alt="Extreme and mean ratio"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "0,0,100",
        title: "Extreme and mean ratio",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;140,180",
            "B;point;free;220,180",
            "L;point;extend;B,A,B,A",
            "LB;line;connect;L,B",
            "Lcirc;circle;radius;L,B;0;0;0;0",
            "Bcirc;circle;radius;B,L;0;0;0;0",
            "MN;line;bichord;Lcirc,Bcirc",
            "M;point;last;MN",
            "N;point;first;MN",
            "MLNarc;sector;sector;B,M,N;0;0;black;0",
            "NBMarc;sector;sector;L,N,M;0;0;black;0",
            "Acirc;circle;radius;A,B;0;0;0;0",
            "LBarc;sector;sector;A,L,B;0;0;black;0",
            "C;point;cutoff;A,N,A,B",
            "Ccirc;circle;radius;C,A;0;0;0;0",
            "Eline;line;bichord;Acirc,Ccirc",
            "E1;point;first;Eline;0",
            "E2;point;last;Eline;0",
            "Carc;sector;sector;C,E2,E1;0;0;black;0",
            "E;point;intersection;A,C,Eline",
            "F;point;cutoff;E,A,E,B",
            "FBarc;sector;sector;E,B,F;0;0;black;0",
            "H;point;cutoff;A,B,A,F",
            "FHarc;sector;sector;A,H,F;0;0;black;0",
            // Hover targets only, for the segments Joyce's construction
            // narrative names but the figure draws only as arcs and
            // radii. Fully transparent — the figure is unchanged.
            "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;0",
            "EB;line;connect;E,B;0;0;0"
        ],
        aliases: { "BA": "AB", "CA": "AC", "BE": "EB", "BL": "LB" },
        // A guide canvas that is itself a procedure, so it carries its own
        // walk (the I.20 / I.47 exception). Joyce narrates it as an ordered
        // list -- "six circles to be drawn, two lines connected, and one line
        // extended" -- and the captions are his sentences, one step each.
        //
        // The invisible carrier circles (Lcirc, Bcirc, Acirc, Ccirc) stay in
        // every set: they draw nothing, and Joyce shows only the arcs of them
        // that matter, which is what the *arc elements are for. Every lettered
        // point here is derived, so none is auto-unioned and each is listed on
        // the slide whose sentence names it -- no deferDraggables needed, only
        // A and B are draggable and both are given.
        slides: (function () {
            var CARRIER = ["Lcirc","Bcirc","Acirc","Ccirc"];
            var GIVEN = CARRIER.concat(["A","B","AB"]);
            var EXT   = GIVEN.concat(["L","LB","LBarc"]);
            var MNC   = EXT.concat(["M","N","MLNarc","NBMarc"]);
            var LINE  = MNC.concat(["MN"]);
            var CUT   = LINE.concat(["C","AC"]);
            var CCIRC = CUT.concat(["E1","E2","Carc","Eline"]);
            var EPT   = CCIRC.concat(["E"]);
            var FPT   = EPT.concat(["F","EB","FBarc"]);
            var DONE  = FPT.concat(["H","FHarc"]);
            return [
              { text: "Altogether, there are six circles to be drawn, two lines connected, and one line extended. In order, they are as follows.",
                visible: GIVEN,
                highlighted: ["AB"] },
              { text: "Extend {BA} and draw a circle centered at {A} with radius {AB} to determine the point {L}.",
                visible: EXT,
                highlighted: ["A"],
                transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                    { elem: "LB", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend },
                    { elem: "LBarc", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
              { text: "Draw circles centered at {B} and {L} with radius {BL} to determine points {M} and {N}.",
                visible: MNC,
                highlighted: ["BL"],
                transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                    { elem: "MLNarc", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                    { elem: "NBMarc", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
              { text: "Draw the straight line {MN}.",
                visible: LINE,
                transition: { animations: [
                    { elem: "MN", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] } },
              { text: "Then {C} is where it meets the circle centered at {A}.",
                visible: CUT,
                highlighted: ["MN","LBarc"],
                transition: { animations: [
                    { elem: "C", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } },
              { text: "Draw a circle centered at {C} with radius {CA}, and connect the two points where it meets the circle centered at {A}.",
                visible: CCIRC,
                highlighted: ["CA"],
                transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                    { elem: "Carc", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                    { elem: "Eline", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] } },
              { text: "Then {E} is where that line meets {AC}.",
                visible: EPT,
                highlighted: ["Eline","AC"],
                transition: { animations: [
                    { elem: "E", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } },
              { text: "Draw the circle centered at {E} with radius {EB} to determine {F}.",
                visible: FPT,
                highlighted: ["EB"],
                transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                    { elem: "FBarc", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                    { elem: "F", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } },
              { text: "Draw the circle centered at {A} with radius {F} to determine {H}, the desired point to cut {AB}.",
                visible: DONE,
                highlighted: ["AB"],
                transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                    { elem: "FHarc", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                    { elem: "H", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } }
            ];
        })()
    });
</script>
</figure>

For the purposes of cutting the line {AB}, the entire diagram does not have to be constructed.  The points {D}, {G}, and {K} are unnecessary.  In the diagram to the right, only those lines and circles necessary for the construction are shown, and only those parts of them that are relevant.

Altogether, there are six circles to be drawn, two lines connected, and one line extended. In order, they are as follows. Extend {BA} and draw a circle centered at {A} with radius {AB} to determine the point {L}. Draw circles centered at {B} and {L} with radius {BL} to determine points {M} and {N}.  Draw the straight line {MN}. Then {C} is where it meets the circle centered at {A}.  Draw a circle centered at {C} with radius {CA}, and connect the two points where it meets the circle centered at {A}.  Then {E} is where that line meets {AC}.  Draw the circle centered at {E} with radius {EB} to determine {F}.  Draw the circle centered at {A} with radius {F} to determine {H}, the desired point to cut {AB}.

#### The golden ratio, the 36°-72°-72° triangle, and regular pentagons

This is the first of several propositions in the *Elements* that treats these concepts. At this point, ratios have not been introduced, so Euclid describes it in basic terms, that a given straight line is cut so that "the rectangle contained by the whole and one of the segments equals the square on the remaining segment."
